From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: idealinsane <idealinsane@localhost>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 18:23:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] rpcserver: keep the NFS proxy crash context per lane

knfsd-syzkaller S1c: a crash in an NFS lane may depend on mutations the
proxy applied to earlier programs of the same lane, because the lane's
knfsd state (clients, sessions, open and lock state) survives between
programs.  The Runner replays the lane's recent programs in order and
joins each one to its MutationDelta on the host journal, by the lane
and the KCOV generation the activation barrier was keyed to.

LastExecuting now keeps, for every record:

- Lane: the proc the program was started on.  Hanged() replaces Proc
  with a synthetic value so pkg/repro keeps hung programs, which loses
  the lane; Lane keeps it.
- NfsProxy: the activation barrier the executor reported in ProgInfo
  (0023), attached when the program's result arrives.  A program still
  running at the crash has none; its generation is the next one its
  lane opened on the host journal.

The crash log prints both on one extra line after the program header,
only for records that have a barrier or a rewritten Proc:

  nfs-proxy: lane=L generation=G program_digest=HEX activation=Acked/0 ...

prog.ParseLog skips the line and none of its keys contains "id=", so
pkg/repro reads the same entries as before; logs without NFS proxy
records are unchanged.

The window bound is job policy; the ring that backs it holds
experimental.nfs_proxy_window programs per proc (1-64, never fewer than
the previous 6).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013uedukxZ18By3FSydgpCRs
